%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level2_TheInvestigation
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_2
  levelNumber: 2
  displayName: The Investigation
  sceneName: Room2_InvestigationOffice
  description: Protecting your personal data and accounts, fake virus pop-ups, what
    to do after a scam, and a message that is real.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 505a3627d9fec30428c0950dace725d0, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 2: THE INVESTIGATION'
  briefingText: 'In this room: protecting your personal data and accounts, fake virus
    pop-ups, what to do after a scam, and a message that is real.

    You''ll get
    6 questions: 4 with answer choices (A-D) and 2 to judge as SCAM or LEGITIMATE.
    Take your time and read each one carefully.'
  caseNotes:
  - While browsing, a full-screen red page appeared and started beeping.
  - 'One minute before this email arrived, you clicked "Forgot password" on NovaCloud
    yourself. Trusted domain (case files): novacloud.example'
  objectives:
  - Answer all 6 questions.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose. Stuck? Press HINT.
  - Score 450+ to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: E07
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A raffle booth at the mall asks you to fill in a form to join. <b>Which
      of these is the biggest privacy warning sign?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: A raffle only needs a way to contact you, not your ID and birthdate.
      Together, those can be used to pretend to be you. The <b>Data Privacy Act</b>
      says they must explain why they collect data and take only what they need.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: name
      field: 5
      match: required
      label: Full name
      finding: A raffle needs to know who won. Asking for a name is reasonable.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: name
    - id: mobile
      field: 5
      match: required
      label: Mobile number
      finding: A raffle needs a way to contact the winner. This is proportionate.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: mobile
    - id: birth
      field: 5
      match: required
      label: Birthdate
      finding: A raffle doesn't need your birthdate. Together with an ID photo it
        can be used for identity theft.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: birth
      part: birth
    - id: id
      field: 5
      match: photo of your valid ID
      label: ID photo
      finding: Far more than joining a raffle needs. An ID photo is valuable to identity
        thieves.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: idphoto
      part: id
    - id: notice
      field: 5
      match: None provided
      label: Privacy notice
      finding: Without a notice you can't know why your data is collected or what
        happens to it (transparency).
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: notice
      part: notice
    - id: free
      field: 5
      match: Free to join!
      label: Free entry
      finding: Free raffles are normal. Not a warning sign on its own.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: info
    - id: shirts
      field: 5
      match: company shirts
      label: Staff
      finding: Uniforms are easy to copy and prove little either way. Not a warning
        sign on its own.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: info
    evidence:
    - id: idphoto
      title: Asks for an ID photo
      whyItMatters: Collect only what is needed (proportionality).
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: birth
      title: Asks for your birthdate
      whyItMatters: Birthdates help thieves pretend to be you.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: notice
      title: No privacy notice
      whyItMatters: People must be told why their data is collected (transparency).
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - They want a photo of your ID and your birthdate just to join, with no notice
      saying why
    - The raffle is free to join
    - The staff wear company shirts
    - The draw is next week
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Too much! An ID photo for a raffle? Ask <b>why they need it</b> and
      look for a privacy notice.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 15: Data
      Privacy Act (RA 10173)"
    hint: Think about what a raffle really needs from you just to contact a winner.
    activity:
      kind: 6
      title: "Mall raffle  \xB7  sign-up form"
      instruction: Check each box on the form. Which ones ask for too much?
      items:
      - key: name
        role: 0
        label: Full name
        text: required
      - key: mobile
        role: 0
        label: Mobile number
        text: required
      - key: birth
        role: 0
        label: Birthdate
        text: required
      - key: id
        role: 0
        label: ID photo
        text: Upload a photo of your valid ID (required)
      - key: notice
        role: 0
        label: Privacy notice
        text: None provided
      - key: info
        role: 8
        label: 
        text: Free to join! Staff in company shirts are at the booth. The draw is
          next week.
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: E08
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A scammer who gets into one account may use it to get into your others.
      <b>Which account needs the strongest protection?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: '"Forgot password" links go to your email. Whoever controls your
      email can reset your other accounts: social media, e-wallets and school. Protect
      it first with a unique password and MFA (a second check, like a code).'
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Your online game account
    - Your main email account, which receives password-reset links
    - Your music streaming account
    - An old forum account you no longer use
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: Your <b>email</b> is the master key. Every "Forgot password" link goes
      there.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 9:
      Information Assets and Asset Management (\"evaluate their value and protection
      requirements\")"
    hint: Where do "Forgot password" links get sent?
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: E09
    channel: 2
    sender: Unknown web page
    senderDetail: 
    subject: SYSTEM SECURITY ALERT
    body: Your computer is INFECTED and LOCKED. Call Certified Support at 1-800-555-0199
      now. DO NOT restart your computer.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: While browsing, a full-screen red page appeared and started
      beeping.
    explanation: This is a <b>tech-support scam</b>. Real warnings come from your
      computer's own security app, not from a web page with a phone number. The "support"
      person will ask for money or control of your PC. Close the tab. If it's stuck,
      close the browser with Task Manager.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: sender
      field: 0
      match: 
      label: The page
      finding: A page you didn't ask for, from a site you don't know.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: 
    - id: alert
      field: 2
      match: 
      label: The alert
      finding: Big red alerts are designed to cause panic.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: 
    - id: locked
      field: 3
      match: INFECTED and LOCKED
      label: The claim
      finding: A web page can't scan your computer. It's only a page made to scare
        you.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: fake
      part: 
    - id: call
      field: 3
      match: Call Certified Support at 1-800-555-0199
      label: What it wants
      finding: Real security warnings never ask you to call a number. This is a tech-support
        scam.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: call
      part: 
    - id: restart
      field: 3
      match: DO NOT restart your computer
      label: The instruction
      finding: It keeps you on the scary page. Closing the browser usually ends it.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: pressure
      part: 
    evidence:
    - id: fake
      title: Fake infection claim
      whyItMatters: Web pages can't see what's on your computer.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: call
      title: Asks you to call 'support'
      whyItMatters: Real warnings don't come with a phone number.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: pressure
      title: Keeps you from closing it
      whyItMatters: Pressure is the scam's main tool.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam! Real alerts come from your <b>computer's settings</b>, never
      from a web page that tells you to call.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Operating System Security
      (\"patch management, and security configuration\")"
    hint: 'Where do real security warnings come from: a web page, or your own computer?'
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: E10
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: "You paid \u20B11,500 to an online seller through PayLite. The seller then
      blocked you and never shipped. <b>What should you do first?</b>"
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Proof is what lets PayLite and the police help you, and the reference
      number can help trace the money. Deleting the chat destroys proof. Public threats
      can get you in trouble. "Pay more to get a refund" is a second scam.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: chat
      field: 5
      match: "Paid na po, \u20B11,500 via PayLite"
      label: Your chat
      finding: The chat shows what was agreed and paid. Keep it - it's evidence.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: chat
      part: m1
    - id: blocked
      field: 5
      match: The seller blocked you
      label: Blocked
      finding: Being blocked after paying is a classic sign of a fake seller. Save
        everything before it disappears.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: blocked
    - id: receipt
      field: 5
      match: reference number
      label: Payment receipt
      finding: The reference number lets PayLite trace where the money went.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: ref
      part: receipt
    - id: profile
      field: 5
      match: profile link and account name
      label: Seller's profile
      finding: The seller's account details help the platform and the police find
        them.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: account
      part: profile
    evidence:
    - id: chat
      title: The chat history
      whyItMatters: Proof of what was agreed. Deleting it destroys evidence.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: ref
      title: Receipt with reference number
      whyItMatters: Reference numbers can help trace the money.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: account
      title: Seller's account details
      whyItMatters: They let the platform and the police act.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Delete the chat so you can move on
    - Post the seller's photo and threaten them publicly
    - Send another payment because they say it "unlocks" your refund
    - Save proof (the chat, the seller's profile and the receipt with its reference
      number), then report to PayLite and the police
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: 'Save the proof first: chat, profile and <b>reference number</b>. Then
      report it.'
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Digital Evidence and Evidence
      Collection (\"collection, preservation, and documentation\")"
    hint: After a scam, what will the e-wallet company and the police ask you to
      show them?
    activity:
      kind: 3
      title: "Marketplace chat  \xB7  seller"
      instruction: Look at what you have. What could prove what happened?
      items:
      - key: m1
        role: 3
        label: You
        text: "Paid na po, \u20B11,500 via PayLite. When will you ship?"
      - key: blocked
        role: 5
        label: BLOCKED
        text: You can no longer message this account. The seller blocked you.
      - key: receipt
        role: 4
        label: payment
        text: "PayLite_Receipt.png  \xB7  \u20B11,500  \xB7  reference number shown"
      - key: profile
        role: 6
        label: seller
        text: Seller's profile link and account name
      minClues: 1
      outcomes:
      - The chat is gone, and with it the proof of what was agreed. Your report becomes
        much harder to act on.
      - The public post starts a fight and can get you into legal trouble. The money
        still isn't traced.
      - The "refund unlock" is a second scam. You lose another payment.
      - PayLite and the authorities get the reference number and account details
        they need to trace the money.
  - id: M01
    channel: 1
    sender: NovaCloud
    senderDetail: no-reply@novacloud.example
    subject: Password reset request
    body: We received a request to reset your password. This link expires in 15 minutes.
      If you didn't request this, you can ignore this email.
    link: "https://novacloud.example/reset?t=8f3k\u2026"
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: 'One minute before this email arrived, you clicked "Forgot password"
      on NovaCloud yourself. Trusted domain (case files): novacloud.example'
    explanation: You asked for this reset yourself a minute ago. The address matches
      the trusted one exactly, and it doesn't ask for your password. It even says
      you can ignore it if you didn't ask. Not every message is a scam!
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: domain
      field: 1
      match: 
      label: Sender address
      finding: no-reply@novacloud.example matches the trusted domain in your case
        files.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
      part: 
    - id: expires
      field: 3
      match: This link expires in 15 minutes.
      label: Expiry
      finding: Short expiry times are normal for reset links - it's a security feature,
        not pressure.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: 
    - id: ignore
      field: 3
      match: If you didn't request this, you can ignore this email.
      label: No pressure
      finding: It lets you ignore it if you didn't ask. A scam would push you to
        act.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: nopressure
      part: 
    - id: link
      field: 4
      match: 
      label: Reset link
      finding: It goes to novacloud.example, the trusted domain - and you asked for
        this reset a minute ago.
      linkAddress: "https://novacloud.example/reset?t=8f3k\u2026"
      clueId: link
      part: 
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: Trusted sender domain
      whyItMatters: Matches novacloud.example in your case files.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: link
      title: Link to the real domain
      whyItMatters: And you requested the reset yourself.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: nopressure
      title: No pressure
      whyItMatters: You can safely ignore it if you didn't ask.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Legitimate! You asked for it, the address matches, and it doesn't ask
      for your password.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Weeks 7\u20138:
      Principles of Information Assurance (authentication)"
    hint: Read the case note. Did you ask for this, and does the address match the
      trusted one?
    activity:
      kind: 0
      title: 
      instruction: 
      items: []
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
  - id: M02
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'A message links to: <b>https://examplebank.example.account-verify.example/login</b>.
      <b>Which is true?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: To find the real website, read the address <b>from right to left</b>,
      up to the first single "/". Here it is <b>account-verify.example</b>. The "examplebank"
      in front is a decoy the scammer added. Nothing is misspelled, and HTTPS doesn't
      make it safe.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables:
    - id: https
      field: 5
      match: https://
      label: https://
      finding: HTTPS only encrypts the connection. It doesn't tell you who owns the
        site.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: addr
    - id: sub
      field: 5
      match: examplebank.example.
      label: The left part
      finding: Everything to the left of the real domain is a subdomain - the attacker
        can write anything there, even a bank's name.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: sub
      part: addr
    - id: domain
      field: 5
      match: account-verify.example
      label: The real domain
      finding: Read the domain from the right, ending just before the first single
        '/'. The real domain is account-verify.example.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: domain
      part: addr
    - id: path
      field: 5
      match: /login
      label: The path
      finding: The part after the '/' can say anything. It doesn't change who owns
        the site.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: addr
    - id: page
      field: 5
      match: ExampleBank Online
      label: The page
      finding: Copying a bank's look is easy. Nothing is misspelled here, so this
        isn't typosquatting either.
      linkAddress: 
      clueId: 
      part: page
    evidence:
    - id: domain
      title: 'Real domain: account-verify.example'
      whyItMatters: The domain decides who owns the page.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    - id: sub
      title: Bank name used as a subdomain
      whyItMatters: Subdomain tricks put a trusted name on the left.
      supportsVerdict: 1
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - It's ExampleBank's site because "examplebank" appears first
    - It's safe because it uses HTTPS
    - 'It''s a subdomain trick: the real domain is account-verify.example'
    - It's a misspelled (typosquatted) bank domain
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: 'Read <b>right to left</b>: the real site is account-verify.example.
      The bank''s name is just a decoy.'
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Web and Application Security"
    hint: Read the web address from right to left, up to the first single slash.
    activity:
      kind: 2
      title: "ExampleBank Online  \xB7  Log in"
      instruction: Check the address bar, piece by piece. Where does this page really
        go?
      items:
      - key: addr
        role: 0
        label: Address
        text: https://examplebank.example.account-verify.example/login
      - key: page
        role: 1
        label: 
        text: ExampleBank Online. Log in to continue.
      - key: button
        role: 7
        label: 
        text: LOG IN
      minClues: 1
      outcomes: []
